var e=JSON.parse(`[{"id":"MCSA-R-001","type":"MCSA-R","skill":"reading","difficulty":2,"timeLimit":180,"instructions":"阅读文章并选择最佳答案。","tags":["science"],"passage":"The human brain contains approximately 86 billion neurons, each connected to thousands of others through synapses. These connections form complex networks that enable everything from basic motor functions to abstract thought. What makes the brain particularly remarkable is its plasticity - the ability to reorganize these neural connections in response to new experiences and learning. This plasticity is most pronounced during childhood but continues throughout adult life, allowing us to acquire new skills and adapt to changing environments.","question":"According to the passage, what allows the brain to learn and adapt throughout life?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The large number of neurons in the brain","isCorrect":false},{"id":"b","text":"The complex networks of neural connections","isCorrect":false},{"id":"c","text":"The brain's plasticity or ability to reorganize connections","isCorrect":true},{"id":"d","text":"The basic motor functions of the body","isCorrect":false}]},{"id":"MCSA-R-002","type":"MCSA-R","skill":"reading","difficulty":2,"timeLimit":180,"instructions":"阅读文章并选择最佳答案。","tags":["business"],"passage":"The concept of remote work has gained unprecedented momentum since 2020. Companies that once insisted on employees being physically present in the office have discovered that productivity can be maintained, and in some cases improved, when staff work from home. However, this shift has also presented challenges, including difficulties in maintaining company culture, reduced opportunities for informal collaboration, and the blurring of boundaries between work and personal life. Many organizations are now adopting hybrid models that combine the flexibility of remote work with the benefits of in-person interaction.","question":"What is the main idea of this passage?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Remote work always increases employee productivity","isCorrect":false},{"id":"b","text":"Companies should require all employees to return to the office","isCorrect":false},{"id":"c","text":"Remote work has benefits and challenges, leading many to adopt hybrid approaches","isCorrect":true},{"id":"d","text":"Company culture is impossible to maintain with remote workers","isCorrect":false}]},{"id":"MCSA-R-003","type":"MCSA-R","skill":"reading","difficulty":2,"timeLimit":120,"instructions":"阅读文章并选择唯一正确答案。","tags":["environment","wildlife"],"passage":"Elephants are the largest land animals on Earth, with African elephants reaching weights of up to six tons. These highly intelligent creatures have complex social structures, living in family groups led by a matriarch, typically the oldest female. Elephants are also known as ecosystem engineers because their feeding habits shape the landscape: they knock down trees to access leaves and bark, which creates clearings that allow grasses and smaller plants to grow. Unfortunately, elephant populations have declined dramatically due to poaching for ivory and habitat loss caused by human expansion.","question":"What is the main reason elephants are called ecosystem engineers?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Their feeding habits create clearings that change the landscape","isCorrect":true},{"id":"b","text":"They can design and build complex structures","isCorrect":false},{"id":"c","text":"They are the largest land animals on Earth","isCorrect":false},{"id":"d","text":"They live in family groups led by a matriarch","isCorrect":false}]},{"id":"MCSA-R-004","type":"MCSA-R","skill":"reading","difficulty":3,"timeLimit":120,"instructions":"阅读文章并选择唯一正确答案。","tags":["history","exploration"],"passage":"The Apollo 11 mission of July 1969 stands as one of humanity's greatest technological achievements. When Neil Armstrong became the first human to set foot on the lunar surface, an estimated 600 million people around the world watched the event on television. The mission was the culmination of a decade-long effort involving hundreds of thousands of engineers, scientists, and support staff. Beyond the symbolic victory in the Space Race, the Apollo program generated countless technological innovations including advances in computing, materials science, and telecommunications that have since found applications in everyday life.","question":"According to the passage, what was one lasting benefit of the Apollo program beyond the moon landing itself?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"It proved that television was a viable global communication medium","isCorrect":false},{"id":"b","text":"It generated technological innovations that found everyday applications","isCorrect":true},{"id":"c","text":"It established that humans could live permanently in space","isCorrect":false},{"id":"d","text":"It ended all political competition between nations","isCorrect":false}]},{"id":"MCSA-R-005","type":"MCSA-R","skill":"reading","difficulty":3,"timeLimit":120,"instructions":"阅读文章并选择唯一正确答案。","tags":["psychology","decision making"],"passage":"Decision fatigue refers to the deteriorating quality of decisions made by an individual after a long session of decision making. Researchers have found that making choices depletes mental energy in a way similar to how physical exertion depletes muscle strength. This phenomenon helps explain why judges tend to grant parole more frequently in the morning than in the afternoon, and why shoppers often make impulsive purchases after spending hours comparing products. One practical strategy for managing decision fatigue is to automate routine decisions, such as what to wear or what to eat, so that mental energy can be reserved for more important matters.","question":"What strategy does the passage suggest for managing decision fatigue?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Making all decisions in the morning when energy is highest","isCorrect":false},{"id":"b","text":"Automating routine decisions to preserve mental energy for important ones","isCorrect":true},{"id":"c","text":"Avoiding all purchases while shopping for long periods","isCorrect":false},{"id":"d","text":"Asking judges to make parole decisions at random times","isCorrect":false}]},{"id":"MCSA-R-006","type":"MCSA-R","skill":"reading","difficulty":2,"timeLimit":120,"instructions":"阅读文章并选择唯一正确答案。","tags":["technology","social media"],"passage":"Social media platforms have transformed the way people communicate, share information, and form communities. On the positive side, these platforms have enabled grassroots social movements, allowed families separated by distance to stay connected, and provided small businesses with affordable marketing channels. However, researchers have also documented negative effects including increased rates of anxiety and depression among heavy users, the rapid spread of misinformation, and the creation of echo chambers that reinforce existing beliefs. The relationship between social media use and mental health is complex and appears to depend on factors such as the type of engagement, the amount of time spent, and individual personality traits.","question":"What is the central idea of this passage?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Social media should be banned due to its negative effects on mental health","isCorrect":false},{"id":"b","text":"Social media only benefits small businesses and social movements","isCorrect":false},{"id":"c","text":"Social media has both positive and negative effects, with a complex relationship to mental health","isCorrect":true},{"id":"d","text":"The main problem with social media is the spread of misinformation","isCorrect":false}]},{"id":"MCSA-R-007","type":"MCSA-R","skill":"reading","difficulty":3,"timeLimit":120,"instructions":"阅读文章并选择唯一正确答案。","tags":["education","technology"],"passage":"The integration of technology into classrooms has been both celebrated and criticized by educators. Proponents highlight how digital tools can personalize learning, allowing students to progress at their own pace and receive immediate feedback on their work. Interactive simulations and educational games can make abstract concepts more concrete and engaging. However, critics point out that technology alone does not improve learning outcomes; the quality of instruction and curriculum design remain the most important factors. Moreover, unequal access to devices and reliable internet connections threatens to widen the achievement gap between affluent and disadvantaged students. The most successful implementations of educational technology are those that complement strong teaching rather than attempting to replace it.","question":"According to the passage, what determines whether educational technology is successful?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The amount of money spent on devices and software","isCorrect":false},{"id":"b","text":"Whether it complements strong teaching rather than replacing it","isCorrect":true},{"id":"c","text":"The number of educational games available to students","isCorrect":false},{"id":"d","text":"Whether all students have the same brand of devices","isCorrect":false}]},{"id":"MCSA-R-008","type":"MCSA-R","skill":"reading","difficulty":2,"timeLimit":120,"instructions":"阅读文章并选择唯一正确答案。","tags":["health","nutrition"],"passage":"Dietary fiber, found in plant-based foods such as fruits, vegetables, whole grains, and legumes, plays an essential role in digestive health. There are two main types: soluble fiber, which dissolves in water and can help lower blood cholesterol and glucose levels, and insoluble fiber, which does not dissolve and helps food move through the digestive system. Adequate fiber intake is associated with a reduced risk of developing conditions such as heart disease, type 2 diabetes, and certain types of colon cancer. Despite these well-documented benefits, most people in developed countries consume far less fiber than the recommended daily amount of 25 to 30 grams.","question":"What does the passage say about the two types of dietary fiber?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Soluble fiber cannot be digested at all","isCorrect":false},{"id":"b","text":"Soluble fiber helps lower blood cholesterol, while insoluble fiber aids food movement through digestion","isCorrect":true},{"id":"c","text":"Both types of fiber perform exactly the same function in the body","isCorrect":false},{"id":"d","text":"Only soluble fiber is found in plant-based foods","isCorrect":false}]},{"id":"MCSA-R-009","type":"MCSA-R","skill":"reading","difficulty":4,"timeLimit":120,"instructions":"阅读文章并选择唯一正确答案。","tags":["economics","global trade"],"passage":"Comparative advantage is a fundamental principle in international trade theory, first articulated by economist David Ricardo in the early 19th century. The principle states that countries benefit from trade even when one country is more efficient at producing all goods, as long as they specialize in producing goods where their relative efficiency advantage is greatest. By focusing on what they produce relatively best and trading for other goods, countries can consume beyond their individual production possibility frontiers. This theory provides the economic rationale for free trade policies and has shaped international economic relations for two centuries, though real-world complications such as tariffs, transportation costs, and strategic industries often modify its pure application.","question":"According to the passage, what is the core insight of the comparative advantage principle?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Countries should only produce goods they can make more efficiently than any other country","isCorrect":false},{"id":"b","text":"Even efficient countries benefit from specializing in what they produce relatively best and trading for other goods","isCorrect":true},{"id":"c","text":"Free trade policies always produce the best outcomes without any complications","isCorrect":false},{"id":"d","text":"Countries should impose tariffs on all imported goods to protect domestic industries","isCorrect":false}]},{"id":"MCSA-R-010","type":"MCSA-R","skill":"reading","difficulty":2,"timeLimit":120,"instructions":"阅读文章并选择唯一正确答案。","tags":["science","ocean"],"passage":"The world's oceans cover more than 70 percent of the Earth's surface and play a crucial role in regulating the planet's climate. Ocean currents act like a global conveyor belt, transporting warm water from the equator toward the poles and cold water back toward the equator. This circulation helps distribute heat around the planet and influences weather patterns including rainfall and temperature. The oceans also absorb approximately one-quarter of the carbon dioxide emitted by human activities, which helps slow climate change but also leads to ocean acidification. Protecting ocean health is therefore essential not only for marine life but for the stability of the entire global climate system.","question":"What dual role do oceans play regarding carbon dioxide, according to the passage?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"They produce carbon dioxide while also releasing oxygen","isCorrect":false},{"id":"b","text":"They absorb carbon dioxide, which helps slow climate change but causes ocean acidification","isCorrect":true},{"id":"c","text":"They store carbon dioxide in deep sea vents permanently","isCorrect":false},{"id":"d","text":"They convert carbon dioxide into food for marine animals","isCorrect":false}]},{"id":"MCSA-R-011","type":"MCSA-R","skill":"reading","difficulty":3,"timeLimit":120,"instructions":"阅读文章并选择唯一正确答案。","tags":["art","literature"],"passage":"The novel as a literary form emerged in the 18th century and quickly became one of the most popular and influential means of storytelling. Unlike earlier forms such as epic poetry or drama, the novel allowed authors to explore the inner lives of characters in unprecedented depth, depicting their thoughts, emotions, and moral development over extended periods. The rise of the novel also coincided with increased literacy rates and the growth of a middle-class readership with both leisure time and disposable income. As the printing industry expanded, novels became more affordable and widely available, transforming reading from an elite activity into a form of mass entertainment and education.","question":"What does the passage identify as a key factor that contributed to the rise of the novel?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The decline of epic poetry and drama","isCorrect":false},{"id":"b","text":"The growth of a middle-class readership and increased literacy rates","isCorrect":true},{"id":"c","text":"Government subsidies for authors and publishers","isCorrect":false},{"id":"d","text":"The invention of the typewriter","isCorrect":false}]},{"id":"MCSA-R-012","type":"MCSA-R","skill":"reading","difficulty":4,"timeLimit":120,"instructions":"阅读文章并选择唯一正确答案。","tags":["politics","law"],"passage":"The doctrine of judicial review, which empowers courts to evaluate the constitutionality of legislative and executive actions, is a cornerstone of many democratic legal systems. In the United States, this power was established by the Supreme Court in the landmark 1803 case Marbury v. Madison, which held that it is emphatically the province of the judiciary to say what the law is. Judicial review serves as a crucial check on the other branches of government, ensuring that their actions remain within the bounds of the constitution. However, the practice has also generated controversy, with critics arguing that unelected judges should not have the final say on matters of public policy. Despite these debates, judicial review remains a fundamental mechanism for protecting constitutional rights and maintaining the rule of law.","question":"What is the primary purpose of judicial review as described in the passage?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"To allow judges to create new legislation when Congress is inactive","isCorrect":false},{"id":"b","text":"To serve as a check on the other branches of government by evaluating constitutionality","isCorrect":true},{"id":"c","text":"To replace the need for a written constitution","isCorrect":false},{"id":"d","text":"To give the executive branch authority over the judiciary","isCorrect":false}]},{"id":"MCSA-R-013","type":"MCSA-R","skill":"reading","difficulty":3,"timeLimit":120,"instructions":"阅读文章并选择唯一正确答案。","tags":["science","archaeology"],"passage":"The discovery of the Dead Sea Scrolls in the mid-20th century was one of the most significant archaeological finds in history. Hidden in caves near the ancient settlement of Qumran, these manuscripts include some of the oldest known copies of books from the Hebrew Bible, dating back more than two thousand years. The scrolls provided scholars with invaluable insights into the religious practices, community organization, and daily life of a Jewish sect from the Second Temple period. Perhaps most importantly, the scrolls demonstrated the remarkable accuracy of later manuscript traditions, confirming that the biblical texts had been transmitted faithfully across centuries of hand-copying.","question":"What was one of the most important findings enabled by the Dead Sea Scrolls, according to the passage?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"They proved that the Hebrew Bible was written in a single generation","isCorrect":false},{"id":"b","text":"They demonstrated the accuracy of later biblical manuscript traditions","isCorrect":true},{"id":"c","text":"They revealed that the Qumran community was the largest in ancient Israel","isCorrect":false},{"id":"d","text":"They contained previously unknown books that were added to the Bible","isCorrect":false}]},{"id":"MCSA-R-014","type":"MCSA-R","skill":"reading","difficulty":2,"timeLimit":120,"instructions":"阅读文章并选择唯一正确答案。","tags":["sports","culture"],"passage":"The Olympic Games originated in ancient Greece, where they were held every four years at Olympia in honor of Zeus. The ancient Olympics featured athletic competitions such as running, wrestling, and chariot racing, drawing participants from city-states across the Greek world. The games were revived in their modern form in 1896, when the first modern Olympics were held in Athens with athletes from 14 nations competing in 43 events. Since then, the Olympics have grown into the world's largest sporting event, featuring thousands of athletes from more than 200 nations. The Olympic movement promotes values of excellence, friendship, and respect, though it has also faced challenges related to doping scandals, political controversies, and the enormous cost of hosting the games.","question":"What does the passage indicate about the relationship between the ancient and modern Olympic Games?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The modern games are an exact replica of the ancient games in every detail","isCorrect":false},{"id":"b","text":"The modern games were inspired by the ancient tradition but have grown into a much larger event","isCorrect":true},{"id":"c","text":"There is no connection between the ancient and modern Olympic Games","isCorrect":false},{"id":"d","text":"The ancient games featured more sports than the modern games","isCorrect":false}]},{"id":"MCSA-R-015","type":"MCSA-R","skill":"reading","difficulty":3,"timeLimit":120,"instructions":"阅读文章并选择唯一正确答案。","tags":["technology","privacy"],"passage":"Encryption is the process of converting information into a code that can only be read by authorized parties who possess the corresponding decryption key. This technology underpins the security of online banking, e-commerce, and private communications. Strong encryption protects individuals from identity theft, financial fraud, and unauthorized surveillance. However, law enforcement agencies have argued that encryption also enables criminals and terrorists to communicate beyond the reach of legal investigation. Proposals to create backdoor access for government agencies have been met with strong opposition from cybersecurity experts, who warn that any vulnerability introduced for law enforcement purposes could also be exploited by malicious actors. The debate between privacy rights and security needs continues to shape encryption policy worldwide.","question":"Why do cybersecurity experts oppose government backdoors in encryption, according to the passage?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Because they believe criminals do not use encryption","isCorrect":false},{"id":"b","text":"Because backdoors could also be exploited by malicious actors","isCorrect":true},{"id":"c","text":"Because encryption has no impact on privacy rights","isCorrect":false},{"id":"d","text":"Because law enforcement already has full access to all communications","isCorrect":false}]},{"id":"MCSA-R-016","type":"MCSA-R","skill":"reading","difficulty":2,"timeLimit":120,"instructions":"阅读文章并选择唯一正确答案。","tags":["geography"],"passage":"The Amazon River is the largest river in the world by volume of water discharged, accounting for approximately one-fifth of the world's total river flow. Originating in the Andes Mountains of Peru, the river winds its way eastward across South America for nearly 6,400 kilometers before emptying into the Atlantic Ocean. The Amazon basin supports the most extensive rainforest on Earth, which is home to an estimated 10 percent of all known species. Despite its immense size and ecological importance, the Amazon ecosystem faces growing threats from deforestation, mining operations, and agricultural expansion that continue to fragment habitats and endanger wildlife.","question":"What is the primary threat to the Amazon ecosystem mentioned in the passage?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Excessive rainfall causing frequent flooding","isCorrect":false},{"id":"b","text":"Deforestation, mining, and agricultural expansion","isCorrect":true},{"id":"c","text":"A decline in the number of fish species in the river","isCorrect":false},{"id":"d","text":"The river changing its course every century","isCorrect":false}]},{"id":"MCSA-R-017","type":"MCSA-R","skill":"reading","difficulty":3,"timeLimit":120,"instructions":"阅读文章并选择唯一正确答案。","tags":["philosophy"],"passage":"The trolley problem is a classic thought experiment in ethics that asks whether it is morally permissible to sacrifice one person to save five others. In the standard scenario, a runaway trolley is headed toward five people tied to the track. You can pull a lever to divert the trolley onto a side track where only one person is tied. Utilitarian reasoning suggests pulling the lever maximizes overall welfare, while deontological perspectives argue that actively causing someone's death, even to save others, violates a fundamental moral rule. This dilemma continues to generate debate among philosophers, psychologists, and ethicists studying human moral reasoning and the design of autonomous systems.","question":"What is the central ethical tension described in the trolley problem?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Whether technology should replace human decision-making in all situations","isCorrect":false},{"id":"b","text":"The conflict between maximizing welfare and adhering to moral rules against directly causing harm","isCorrect":true},{"id":"c","text":"Whether five lives are inherently more valuable than one life in every circumstance","isCorrect":false},{"id":"d","text":"The question of who should be responsible for maintaining trolley safety standards","isCorrect":false}]},{"id":"MCSA-R-018","type":"MCSA-R","skill":"reading","difficulty":2,"timeLimit":120,"instructions":"阅读文章并选择唯一正确答案。","tags":["music"],"passage":"Beethoven composed some of his most celebrated works after he had become almost completely deaf. His Ninth Symphony, completed in 1824, broke new ground by incorporating vocal soloists and a chorus into the final movement, a revolutionary choice for a symphonic work at the time. The symphony's famous 'Ode to Joy' finale has become an anthem of universal brotherhood, performed at momentous occasions around the world. Beethoven's ability to create music of extraordinary complexity and emotional depth without being able to hear it demonstrates the remarkable capacity of the human imagination and the power of internalized musical understanding.","question":"What was revolutionary about Beethoven's Ninth Symphony?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"It was the first symphony ever composed entirely in silence","isCorrect":false},{"id":"b","text":"It incorporated vocal soloists and a chorus into a symphonic work","isCorrect":true},{"id":"c","text":"It was performed exclusively outdoors in public squares","isCorrect":false},{"id":"d","text":"It used electronic instruments for the first time in classical music","isCorrect":false}]},{"id":"MCSA-R-019","type":"MCSA-R","skill":"reading","difficulty":4,"timeLimit":120,"instructions":"阅读文章并选择唯一正确答案。","tags":["biology"],"passage":"Epigenetics is the study of heritable changes in gene expression that do not involve alterations to the underlying DNA sequence. Through mechanisms such as DNA methylation and histone modification, environmental factors including diet, stress, and toxin exposure can effectively switch genes on or off. These epigenetic markers can persist across cell divisions and, in some cases, be passed from parent to offspring. This challenges the traditional view that inheritance is determined solely by DNA sequence, suggesting that life experiences can have intergenerational biological consequences. Research in this field has profound implications for understanding diseases ranging from cancer to psychiatric disorders.","question":"What does epigenetics challenge about traditional views of inheritance?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The idea that all traits are determined exclusively by environmental factors","isCorrect":false},{"id":"b","text":"The belief that inheritance is determined solely by DNA sequence, as life experiences may also have intergenerational effects","isCorrect":true},{"id":"c","text":"The theory that genes are located on chromosomes within the cell nucleus","isCorrect":false},{"id":"d","text":"The concept that DNA has a double helix structure","isCorrect":false}]},{"id":"MCSA-R-020","type":"MCSA-R","skill":"reading","difficulty":2,"timeLimit":120,"instructions":"阅读文章并选择唯一正确答案。","tags":["architecture"],"passage":"The ancient Romans were master builders whose engineering innovations transformed architecture across their vast empire. They perfected the use of concrete, which allowed them to construct structures of unprecedented scale and complexity, including the Pantheon's massive dome that remained the largest in the world for over a millennium. Roman architects also developed the arch and the vault, which distributed weight more efficiently than the post-and-lintel systems used by earlier civilizations. These technical advances, combined with organizational skill and abundant labor, enabled the construction of aqueducts, amphitheaters, and roads that served as the infrastructure backbone of Roman civilization.","question":"What material innovation enabled Romans to build at unprecedented scale?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The development of steel-reinforced framing systems","isCorrect":false},{"id":"b","text":"The perfection of concrete as a building material","isCorrect":true},{"id":"c","text":"The invention of the elevator for tall structures","isCorrect":false},{"id":"d","text":"The use of prefabricated modular components","isCorrect":false}]},{"id":"MCSA-R-021","type":"MCSA-R","skill":"reading","difficulty":3,"timeLimit":120,"instructions":"阅读文章并选择唯一正确答案。","tags":["linguistics"],"passage":"Pidgin languages emerge when speakers of mutually unintelligible languages need to communicate for purposes such as trade or labor. These languages typically have simplified grammar and limited vocabulary drawn from multiple source languages. When a pidgin becomes the native language of a community and children acquire it as their first tongue, it develops into a creole with expanded vocabulary and more complex grammatical structures. This process of creolization demonstrates the human capacity for language creation and has provided linguists with insights into how languages naturally evolve and develop complexity over generations, often within remarkably short periods of time.","question":"What distinguishes a creole from a pidgin according to the passage?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Creoles are spoken only in trading contexts while pidgins are used in all situations","isCorrect":false},{"id":"b","text":"Creoles have expanded vocabulary and complex grammar, having become native languages acquired by children","isCorrect":true},{"id":"c","text":"Pidgins are more grammatically complex than creoles in every measurable aspect","isCorrect":false},{"id":"d","text":"Creoles can only emerge in island communities isolated from mainland influences","isCorrect":false}]},{"id":"MCSA-R-022","type":"MCSA-R","skill":"reading","difficulty":2,"timeLimit":120,"instructions":"阅读文章并选择唯一正确答案。","tags":["health"],"passage":"Sleep plays a vital role in physical health, cognitive function, and emotional well-being. During deep sleep, the body repairs tissues, builds bone and muscle, and strengthens the immune system. The brain consolidates memories and clears metabolic waste products through the glymphatic system, which is most active during sleep. Chronic sleep deprivation has been linked to an increased risk of cardiovascular disease, diabetes, depression, and impaired immune function. Despite these well-established findings, surveys consistently show that a significant proportion of adults in industrialized nations fail to obtain the recommended seven to nine hours of sleep per night.","question":"What does the brain accomplish during sleep according to the passage?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"It completely shuts down all neural activity to conserve energy","isCorrect":false},{"id":"b","text":"It consolidates memories and clears metabolic waste products through the glymphatic system","isCorrect":true},{"id":"c","text":"It processes only negative emotions while discarding positive memories","isCorrect":false},{"id":"d","text":"It generates new neurons at a rate faster than during any waking period","isCorrect":false}]},{"id":"MCSA-R-023","type":"MCSA-R","skill":"reading","difficulty":4,"timeLimit":120,"instructions":"阅读文章并选择唯一正确答案。","tags":["physics"],"passage":"General relativity, published by Albert Einstein in 1915, reconceptualized gravity not as a force acting at a distance, as Newton had described it, but as the curvature of spacetime caused by mass and energy. According to this framework, massive objects like the Sun warp the fabric of spacetime around them, and planets follow curved paths not because they are pulled by a force, but because they are moving along the shortest paths through this curved geometry. General relativity has passed every experimental test to date, predicting phenomena such as the bending of starlight around the Sun, the precession of Mercury's orbit, and more recently, gravitational waves detected from colliding black holes billions of light-years away.","question":"How does general relativity differ from Newton's conception of gravity?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"It treats gravity as a force transmitted instantaneously across any distance","isCorrect":false},{"id":"b","text":"It describes gravity as the curvature of spacetime caused by mass and energy, rather than a force acting at a distance","isCorrect":true},{"id":"c","text":"It argues that gravity only exists within the solar system and not elsewhere in the universe","isCorrect":false},{"id":"d","text":"It proves that gravity is an illusion and objects move randomly through space","isCorrect":false}]},{"id":"MCSA-R-024","type":"MCSA-R","skill":"reading","difficulty":2,"timeLimit":120,"instructions":"阅读文章并选择唯一正确答案。","tags":["agriculture"],"passage":"The Green Revolution of the mid-20th century dramatically increased global food production through the introduction of high-yielding crop varieties, synthetic fertilizers, and improved irrigation techniques. Countries such as India and Mexico, which had previously faced chronic food shortages, achieved self-sufficiency in staple grain production. However, the Green Revolution also brought environmental costs, including soil degradation, water depletion, and reduced agricultural biodiversity as farmers abandoned traditional crop varieties in favor of a narrow range of high-yield cultivars. Current agricultural research seeks to build on the productivity gains of the Green Revolution while addressing its environmental limitations through sustainable intensification.","question":"What is identified as a negative consequence of the Green Revolution?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"A permanent decline in global food production across all regions","isCorrect":false},{"id":"b","text":"Soil degradation, water depletion, and reduced agricultural biodiversity","isCorrect":true},{"id":"c","text":"The complete abandonment of agriculture in developing countries","isCorrect":false},{"id":"d","text":"An increase in global food prices that made staples unaffordable","isCorrect":false}]},{"id":"MCSA-R-025","type":"MCSA-R","skill":"reading","difficulty":3,"timeLimit":120,"instructions":"阅读文章并选择唯一正确答案。","tags":["sociology"],"passage":"The concept of the sociological imagination, introduced by C. Wright Mills, describes the ability to connect personal troubles with broader social structures and historical forces. Rather than viewing individual experiences such as unemployment or marital difficulties as purely personal failures, the sociological imagination encourages examining how economic conditions, cultural norms, and institutional arrangements shape personal outcomes. This perspective enables individuals to understand their biographies within the context of history and society, transforming private concerns into public issues. Mills argued that developing this capacity is essential for both understanding society and engaging in effective democratic citizenship.","question":"What does the sociological imagination enable according to the passage?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The ability to ignore social problems and focus exclusively on personal success","isCorrect":false},{"id":"b","text":"Connecting personal troubles with broader social structures and historical forces","isCorrect":true},{"id":"c","text":"Predicting individual behavior with perfect accuracy using statistical models","isCorrect":false},{"id":"d","text":"Eliminating all forms of social inequality through governmental policy","isCorrect":false}]},{"id":"MCSA-R-026","type":"MCSA-R","skill":"reading","difficulty":2,"timeLimit":120,"instructions":"阅读文章并选择唯一正确答案。","tags":["engineering"],"passage":"The Panama Canal, completed in 1914, stands as one of the greatest engineering achievements of the modern era. By cutting through the narrow Isthmus of Panama, the canal eliminated the need for ships to make the lengthy and dangerous journey around the southern tip of South America. The construction required overcoming immense challenges including tropical diseases, difficult terrain, and the need for an innovative system of locks to raise and lower ships across the continental divide. Today, the canal continues to serve as a vital artery of global trade, with an expanded set of locks completed in 2016 accommodating the largest modern cargo vessels.","question":"What was the primary benefit of the Panama Canal when it was completed?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"It allowed ships to avoid the lengthy journey around the southern tip of South America","isCorrect":true},{"id":"b","text":"It created the world's largest artificial lake for recreational purposes","isCorrect":false},{"id":"c","text":"It connected the Atlantic Ocean directly to the Indian Ocean","isCorrect":false},{"id":"d","text":"It provided a permanent land bridge between North and South America","isCorrect":false}]},{"id":"MCSA-R-027","type":"MCSA-R","skill":"reading","difficulty":4,"timeLimit":120,"instructions":"阅读文章并选择唯一正确答案。","tags":["history","economics"],"passage":"The transition from feudalism to capitalism in Western Europe was neither rapid nor uniform, occurring over several centuries through a complex interplay of economic, political, and social transformations. The enclosure movement in England, which privatized commonly held lands, displaced large numbers of peasants from rural areas, creating a mobile labor force that would eventually supply the factories of the Industrial Revolution. Simultaneously, overseas colonization and the expansion of trade generated unprecedented accumulations of capital, while new legal frameworks increasingly protected private property and commercial contracts. Together, these developments dismantled the reciprocal obligations that had characterized feudal relations and established market-based systems of production and exchange.","question":"What role did the enclosure movement play in the transition to capitalism?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"It strengthened feudal obligations between lords and peasants","isCorrect":false},{"id":"b","text":"It displaced peasants from rural areas, creating a mobile labor force for industrial factories","isCorrect":true},{"id":"c","text":"It reinforced the system of commonly held agricultural lands","isCorrect":false},{"id":"d","text":"It primarily benefited overseas colonies rather than affecting England itself","isCorrect":false}]},{"id":"MCSA-R-028","type":"MCSA-R","skill":"reading","difficulty":2,"timeLimit":120,"instructions":"阅读文章并选择唯一正确答案。","tags":["psychology"],"passage":"Positive psychology emerged as a distinct field in the late 1990s, focusing on what makes life worth living rather than solely on treating mental illness. Researchers in this field study concepts such as happiness, flow, gratitude, and resilience to understand how individuals and communities can flourish. Unlike traditional psychology, which often concentrates on pathology and dysfunction, positive psychology seeks to identify and cultivate the strengths and virtues that enable people to thrive. Interventions based on positive psychology principles, such as keeping a gratitude journal or practicing mindfulness, have been shown to improve well-being and reduce symptoms of depression in numerous controlled studies.","question":"How does positive psychology differ from traditional psychology?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"It focuses exclusively on severe mental illness and pathology","isCorrect":false},{"id":"b","text":"It concentrates on strengths, virtues, and flourishing rather than primarily on dysfunction and pathology","isCorrect":true},{"id":"c","text":"It rejects all scientific methods in favor of philosophical speculation","isCorrect":false},{"id":"d","text":"It argues that all negative emotions should be completely suppressed","isCorrect":false}]},{"id":"MCSA-R-029","type":"MCSA-R","skill":"reading","difficulty":3,"timeLimit":120,"instructions":"阅读文章并选择唯一正确答案。","tags":["environment","chemistry"],"passage":"The Montreal Protocol, signed in 1987, is widely regarded as one of the most successful international environmental agreements in history. The treaty phased out the production of chlorofluorocarbons and other substances responsible for depleting the Earth's protective ozone layer. What made the protocol particularly effective was its binding targets, its flexibility in allowing different timelines for developing countries, and a multilateral fund that provided financial assistance for the transition to safer alternatives. Scientific monitoring has confirmed that the ozone layer is now recovering, with projections suggesting it could return to pre-1980 levels by the middle of the 21st century.","question":"What factors contributed to the Montreal Protocol's success?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Voluntary guidelines with no enforcement mechanisms or financial support","isCorrect":false},{"id":"b","text":"Binding targets, flexible timelines for developing countries, and a multilateral fund for alternatives","isCorrect":true},{"id":"c","text":"Immediate and complete bans on all industrial production worldwide","isCorrect":false},{"id":"d","text":"Exclusive focus on developed nations while ignoring developing country participation","isCorrect":false}]},{"id":"MCSA-R-030","type":"MCSA-R","skill":"reading","difficulty":2,"timeLimit":120,"instructions":"阅读文章并选择唯一正确答案。","tags":["film"],"passage":"The transition from silent films to talkies in the late 1920s fundamentally transformed the film industry. Many silent film stars, whose careers had been built on expressive physical performance, found themselves unable to adapt to the demands of spoken dialogue, and their popularity rapidly declined. The technology required for synchronized sound necessitated expensive retooling of theaters and studios, consolidating power among the major studios that could afford the investment. The arrival of sound also influenced filmmaking aesthetics, as cameras became more static in early talkies due to the need to house noisy equipment in soundproof booths, temporarily reducing the visual dynamism that silent cinema had developed.","question":"How did the transition to talkies affect silent film stars?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"All silent film stars immediately became more popular with the addition of their voices","isCorrect":false},{"id":"b","text":"Many could not adapt to spoken dialogue and saw their careers decline","isCorrect":true},{"id":"c","text":"Silent film stars were exclusively hired to direct talking pictures","isCorrect":false},{"id":"d","text":"The transition had no effect on actors' careers whatsoever","isCorrect":false}]},{"id":"MCSA-R-031","type":"MCSA-R","skill":"reading","difficulty":4,"timeLimit":120,"instructions":"阅读文章并选择唯一正确答案。","tags":["anthropology"],"passage":"Marcel Mauss's concept of the gift economy, articulated in his 1925 essay The Gift, challenges the assumption that economic exchange is fundamentally driven by individual self-interest. Through comparative analysis of gift-giving practices in Polynesian, Melanesian, and Native American societies, Mauss demonstrated that gifts create obligations: the obligation to give, the obligation to receive, and the obligation to reciprocate. Far from being altruistic acts without expectation of return, gifts in these societies function as mechanisms for establishing alliances, negotiating status, and maintaining social cohesion. Mauss's analysis continues to influence contemporary debates about the nature of economic rationality and the possibility of alternative economic arrangements.","question":"What does Mauss argue about the nature of gifts in the societies he studied?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Gifts are purely altruistic acts with no expectation of any return","isCorrect":false},{"id":"b","text":"Gifts create obligations to give, receive, and reciprocate, functioning as social mechanisms","isCorrect":true},{"id":"c","text":"Gift-giving has no economic or social significance in traditional societies","isCorrect":false},{"id":"d","text":"Gifts are only exchanged between enemies as a form of ritualized warfare","isCorrect":false}]},{"id":"MCSA-R-032","type":"MCSA-R","skill":"reading","difficulty":2,"timeLimit":120,"instructions":"阅读文章并选择唯一正确答案。","tags":["astronomy"],"passage":"Mars has long captured human imagination as a potential abode for life beyond Earth. Robotic explorers have revealed a world that was once much warmer and wetter than it is today, with evidence of ancient rivers, lakes, and possibly oceans. Current missions continue to search for signs of past microbial life while studying the planet's geology and climate. NASA's Perseverance rover, which landed in 2021, is collecting rock samples that future missions may return to Earth for detailed analysis. These efforts represent incremental steps toward answering one of humanity's most profound questions: whether life has ever existed elsewhere in our solar system.","question":"What evidence suggests Mars was once different from its current state?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Photographs of current rainfall and vegetation on the Martian surface","isCorrect":false},{"id":"b","text":"Evidence of ancient rivers, lakes, and possibly oceans, indicating a warmer and wetter past","isCorrect":true},{"id":"c","text":"Fossilized remains of large animals discovered by early Mars landers","isCorrect":false},{"id":"d","text":"Historical records from ancient civilizations describing contact with Martians","isCorrect":false}]},{"id":"MCSA-R-033","type":"MCSA-R","skill":"reading","difficulty":3,"timeLimit":120,"instructions":"阅读文章并选择唯一正确答案。","tags":["law"],"passage":"The concept of habeas corpus, Latin for 'you shall have the body,' is a fundamental legal protection against arbitrary detention that originated in medieval England. This legal writ requires that a person under arrest be brought before a court to determine whether their detention is lawful. If the court finds insufficient legal justification, it can order the person's release. Habeas corpus has been described as the great writ of liberty, serving as a crucial safeguard against executive overreach. While many constitutions guarantee this right, its suspension during emergencies has been a recurring point of legal and political controversy throughout modern history.","question":"What is the primary function of habeas corpus?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"To automatically convict individuals accused of serious crimes","isCorrect":false},{"id":"b","text":"To require that a detained person be brought before a court to determine the lawfulness of their detention","isCorrect":true},{"id":"c","text":"To transfer legal authority from courts to executive agencies","isCorrect":false},{"id":"d","text":"To permanently release all prisoners regardless of their legal status","isCorrect":false}]},{"id":"MCSA-R-034","type":"MCSA-R","skill":"reading","difficulty":2,"timeLimit":120,"instructions":"阅读文章并选择唯一正确答案。","tags":["culinary history"],"passage":"Tea originated in China, where according to legend, the Emperor Shen Nong discovered its refreshing properties when leaves from a nearby tree blew into his pot of boiling water around 2737 BCE. From China, tea cultivation and consumption spread throughout East Asia, with Japan developing its own highly ritualized tea ceremony that elevated the preparation and serving of tea to an art form. European traders introduced tea to the West in the 16th century, where it became enormously popular, particularly in Britain. The British taste for tea had far-reaching consequences, including the establishment of vast tea plantations in India and Ceylon, and famously, the Boston Tea Party protest against British taxation.","question":"How did tea spread globally according to the passage?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"It originated in Britain and spread eastward to China and Japan through colonial expansion","isCorrect":false},{"id":"b","text":"It originated in China, spread through East Asia, and was introduced to the West by European traders in the 16th century","isCorrect":true},{"id":"c","text":"It was simultaneously discovered on every continent during the same historical period","isCorrect":false},{"id":"d","text":"It was brought to China by European missionaries who had learned about it in India","isCorrect":false}]},{"id":"MCSA-R-035","type":"MCSA-R","skill":"reading","difficulty":4,"timeLimit":120,"instructions":"阅读文章并选择唯一正确答案。","tags":["political science"],"passage":"Gerrymandering refers to the manipulation of electoral district boundaries to advantage a particular political party or group. The term originated in 1812 when Massachusetts Governor Elbridge Gerry approved a salamander-shaped district designed to benefit his party. Modern gerrymandering employs sophisticated data analytics to draw districts that pack opposition voters into a small number of districts or crack them across many districts to dilute their voting power. While courts have struck down racial gerrymandering as unconstitutional, partisan gerrymandering remains a contentious legal and political issue. Critics argue it undermines democratic representation by making seats uncompetitive and reducing the responsiveness of elected officials to their constituents.","question":"What is the effect of cracking in gerrymandering?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Concentrating opposition voters in a single district to limit their influence elsewhere","isCorrect":false},{"id":"b","text":"Spreading opposition voters across many districts to dilute their voting power in each","isCorrect":true},{"id":"c","text":"Creating entirely new voting rules that require unanimous agreement","isCorrect":false},{"id":"d","text":"Eliminating voting entirely in districts that historically support the opposition","isCorrect":false}]},{"id":"MCSA-R-036","type":"MCSA-R","skill":"reading","difficulty":2,"timeLimit":120,"instructions":"阅读文章并选择唯一正确答案。","tags":["dance"],"passage":"Ballet originated in the Italian Renaissance courts of the 15th century before being developed into a formalized art in France under the patronage of King Louis XIV, who founded the Royal Academy of Dance in 1661. The codification of ballet positions and technique during this period established the foundational vocabulary that all subsequent ballet training would build upon. In the 19th century, pointe work revolutionized the art form, allowing female dancers to appear weightless and ethereal. The Romantic era produced beloved works like Giselle, while Russian choreographers of the late 19th and early 20th centuries created the classical repertoire including Swan Lake and The Nutcracker that remain cornerstones of ballet companies worldwide.","question":"Where did ballet originate according to the passage?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"In Russian imperial theaters during the 19th century","isCorrect":false},{"id":"b","text":"In the Italian Renaissance courts of the 15th century","isCorrect":true},{"id":"c","text":"In American dance studios of the early 20th century","isCorrect":false},{"id":"d","text":"In ancient Greek ceremonial performances","isCorrect":false}]},{"id":"MCSA-R-037","type":"MCSA-R","skill":"reading","difficulty":3,"timeLimit":120,"instructions":"阅读文章并选择唯一正确答案。","tags":["veterinary science"],"passage":"Zoonotic diseases, which can be transmitted from animals to humans, account for a significant proportion of emerging infectious diseases. Pathogens may jump species barriers when humans encroach on wildlife habitats, through agricultural practices that bring livestock into close contact with wildlife, or via the wildlife trade. Examples of zoonotic diseases include rabies, Ebola, and certain strains of influenza. The One Health approach, which recognizes the interconnection between human health, animal health, and environmental health, has gained traction as a framework for preventing and responding to zoonotic threats. This approach emphasizes collaboration between physicians, veterinarians, ecologists, and public health professionals to address health challenges at the human-animal-environment interface.","question":"What is the One Health approach described in the passage?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"A framework that focuses exclusively on treating diseases in human patients","isCorrect":false},{"id":"b","text":"An approach recognizing the interconnection between human health, animal health, and environmental health","isCorrect":true},{"id":"c","text":"A method that involves treating only animals to prevent all human diseases","isCorrect":false},{"id":"d","text":"A policy that eliminates all contact between humans and animals","isCorrect":false}]},{"id":"MCSA-R-038","type":"MCSA-R","skill":"reading","difficulty":2,"timeLimit":120,"instructions":"阅读文章并选择唯一正确答案。","tags":["meteorology"],"passage":"Lightning is a powerful natural electrical discharge that occurs during thunderstorms. It is caused by the buildup of electrical charges within clouds, where ice particles collide and transfer charge, with lighter positively charged particles rising to the top of the cloud and heavier negatively charged particles sinking to the bottom. When the electrical potential between the cloud and the ground or between different parts of the cloud becomes large enough, a lightning strike occurs, briefly heating the surrounding air to temperatures hotter than the surface of the Sun. Thunder results from the rapid expansion of this superheated air, which creates a shock wave that we hear as a loud rumble or crack.","question":"What causes the sound of thunder according to the passage?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The collision of rain droplets within the cloud","isCorrect":false},{"id":"b","text":"The rapid expansion of superheated air creating a shock wave","isCorrect":true},{"id":"c","text":"The sound of electricity itself traveling through the air","isCorrect":false},{"id":"d","text":"The echo of lightning bouncing off nearby mountains","isCorrect":false}]},{"id":"MCSA-R-039","type":"MCSA-R","skill":"reading","difficulty":4,"timeLimit":120,"instructions":"阅读文章并选择唯一正确答案。","tags":["neuroscience"],"passage":"The default mode network is a set of interconnected brain regions that becomes active when individuals are not focused on external tasks, engaging instead in internally directed thought such as daydreaming, remembering the past, or planning the future. Discovered through functional neuroimaging, this network challenges the earlier assumption that the brain is simply at rest during unfocused states. Research has linked atypical default mode network activity to various psychiatric and neurological conditions including depression, anxiety, and Alzheimer's disease. Understanding how this network functions and dysfunctions may provide new targets for therapeutic interventions and offers insight into the fundamental operations of the human mind during its most introspective states.","question":"What does the default mode network do according to the passage?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"It becomes active during focused external tasks and deactivates during rest","isCorrect":false},{"id":"b","text":"It engages during internally directed thought such as daydreaming, remembering, and planning","isCorrect":true},{"id":"c","text":"It controls only basic motor functions like breathing and heartbeat","isCorrect":false},{"id":"d","text":"It has been shown to have no connection to any psychiatric or neurological conditions","isCorrect":false}]},{"id":"MCSA-R-040","type":"MCSA-R","skill":"reading","difficulty":2,"timeLimit":120,"instructions":"阅读文章并选择唯一正确答案。","tags":["photography"],"passage":"The daguerreotype, invented by Louis Daguerre in 1839, was the first commercially successful photographic process. Each daguerreotype was a unique image produced on a silver-coated copper plate, yielding remarkable detail and a luminous quality that distinguished it from later paper-based processes. The invention caused a sensation, with the French government acquiring the patent and making it freely available to the world. Portrait studios proliferated rapidly, making it possible for middle-class families to possess likenesses of their loved ones for the first time in history. However, the daguerreotype's limitation to a single copy and its fragility eventually led to its replacement by negative-positive processes that allowed multiple prints.","question":"Why was the daguerreotype eventually replaced?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The French government revoked the patent and banned its use","isCorrect":false},{"id":"b","text":"It produced only a single copy, and its fragility was impractical compared to negative-positive processes","isCorrect":true},{"id":"c","text":"It could only capture images in complete darkness","isCorrect":false},{"id":"d","text":"Portrait studios refused to use the technology due to its high cost","isCorrect":false}]},{"id":"MCSA-R-041","type":"MCSA-R","skill":"reading","difficulty":3,"timeLimit":120,"instructions":"阅读文章并选择唯一正确答案。","tags":["materials science"],"passage":"Graphene is a single layer of carbon atoms arranged in a two-dimensional honeycomb lattice, making it the thinnest material known to science. Despite being just one atom thick, graphene is approximately 200 times stronger than steel by weight, exhibits exceptional electrical and thermal conductivity, and is nearly transparent. These extraordinary properties have generated intense research interest across fields ranging from electronics to medicine. Potential applications include flexible touchscreens, ultra-efficient solar cells, improved water filtration membranes, and novel medical sensors. However, challenges in manufacturing high-quality graphene at commercial scales and costs have limited its widespread adoption thus far.","question":"What is the primary obstacle to graphene's widespread adoption?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Its lack of any useful properties compared to conventional materials","isCorrect":false},{"id":"b","text":"Challenges in manufacturing high-quality graphene at commercial scales and costs","isCorrect":true},{"id":"c","text":"Concerns that graphene is radioactive and dangerous to handle","isCorrect":false},{"id":"d","text":"Legal restrictions that prohibit the use of carbon-based materials","isCorrect":false}]},{"id":"MCSA-R-042","type":"MCSA-R","skill":"reading","difficulty":2,"timeLimit":120,"instructions":"阅读文章并选择唯一正确答案。","tags":["oceanography"],"passage":"The Mariana Trench, located in the western Pacific Ocean, is the deepest known point in Earth's oceans, reaching a depth of approximately 11,000 meters at its lowest point, the Challenger Deep. The immense pressure at such depths, over 1,000 times standard atmospheric pressure, creates an environment so extreme that only specially designed submersibles can reach it. Despite the darkness, freezing temperatures, and crushing pressure, exploration has revealed that life thrives even here, with unique organisms adapted to these harsh conditions. The first manned descent to the Challenger Deep was accomplished in 1960 by Jacques Piccard and Don Walsh, and only a handful of missions have reached this depth since.","question":"What surprising discovery has been made in the Mariana Trench?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The trench is actually shallower than previously measured","isCorrect":false},{"id":"b","text":"Life thrives there despite extreme darkness, pressure, and cold temperatures","isCorrect":true},{"id":"c","text":"The trench contains breathable air pockets at its deepest points","isCorrect":false},{"id":"d","text":"Ancient human artifacts have been found on the trench floor","isCorrect":false}]},{"id":"MCSA-R-043","type":"MCSA-R","skill":"reading","difficulty":4,"timeLimit":120,"instructions":"阅读文章并选择唯一正确答案。","tags":["philosophy","ethics"],"passage":"Utilitarianism, as developed by Jeremy Bentham and John Stuart Mill, holds that the morally right action is the one that produces the greatest happiness for the greatest number of people. Bentham proposed a felicific calculus to quantify pleasure and pain across different dimensions including intensity, duration, certainty, and extent. Mill refined the theory by distinguishing between higher pleasures of the intellect and lower pleasures of mere sensation, arguing famously that it is better to be a human being dissatisfied than a pig satisfied. Critics of utilitarianism contend that it cannot adequately account for individual rights and justice, as it could theoretically justify sacrificing innocent individuals if doing so produced greater overall happiness.","question":"What is a key criticism of utilitarianism mentioned in the passage?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"It focuses too much on individual rights at the expense of collective welfare","isCorrect":false},{"id":"b","text":"It could theoretically justify sacrificing innocent individuals if doing so produced greater overall happiness","isCorrect":true},{"id":"c","text":"It completely ignores the consequences of actions in moral reasoning","isCorrect":false},{"id":"d","text":"It was developed exclusively for economic analysis and has no ethical dimension","isCorrect":false}]},{"id":"MCSA-R-044","type":"MCSA-R","skill":"reading","difficulty":2,"timeLimit":120,"instructions":"阅读文章并选择唯一正确答案。","tags":["chemistry"],"passage":"Water is unique among compounds for having several anomalous properties that are essential for life as we know it. Unlike most substances, water expands when it freezes, which is why ice floats on liquid water. This property ensures that lakes and rivers freeze from the top down, allowing aquatic life to survive winter beneath the insulating ice layer. Water also has an exceptionally high specific heat capacity, meaning it can absorb or release large amounts of heat with relatively small changes in temperature, which helps moderate Earth's climate and stabilizes the internal temperature of living organisms.","question":"Why is water's expansion upon freezing important for aquatic life?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"It causes lakes to freeze from the bottom up, forcing fish to the surface","isCorrect":false},{"id":"b","text":"It allows ice to float and insulate the water below, enabling aquatic life to survive winter","isCorrect":true},{"id":"c","text":"It prevents any ice from forming on bodies of water whatsoever","isCorrect":false},{"id":"d","text":"It has no effect on aquatic life, only affecting terrestrial organisms","isCorrect":false}]},{"id":"MCSA-R-045","type":"MCSA-R","skill":"reading","difficulty":3,"timeLimit":120,"instructions":"阅读文章并选择唯一正确答案。","tags":["urban planning"],"passage":"The concept of the 15-minute city proposes that all essential services including work, shopping, education, healthcare, and leisure should be accessible within a 15-minute walk or bike ride from any resident's home. Championed by urbanist Carlos Moreno, this model aims to reduce car dependency, lower carbon emissions, and improve quality of life by returning the neighborhood to the center of urban experience. Implementation requires a fundamental rethinking of urban zoning, moving away from the segregation of residential, commercial, and industrial areas toward mixed-use development. Several cities including Paris, Melbourne, and Portland have begun incorporating 15-minute city principles into their urban planning strategies.","question":"What is the core idea of the 15-minute city?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"All residents must travel exactly 15 minutes to reach any destination","isCorrect":false},{"id":"b","text":"Essential services should be accessible within a 15-minute walk or bike ride from home","isCorrect":true},{"id":"c","text":"Cities should be designed so that all commutes take exactly 15 minutes by car","isCorrect":false},{"id":"d","text":"Neighborhoods should be segregated into distinct zones based on activity type","isCorrect":false}]},{"id":"MCSA-R-046","type":"MCSA-R","skill":"reading","difficulty":2,"timeLimit":120,"instructions":"阅读文章并选择唯一正确答案。","tags":["paleontology"],"passage":"The extinction of the dinosaurs approximately 66 million years ago is now widely attributed to a massive asteroid impact that struck what is today the Yucatán Peninsula in Mexico. The impact created the Chicxulub crater, measuring over 150 kilometers in diameter, and ejected enormous quantities of dust and sulfur compounds into the atmosphere. This debris blocked sunlight for months or years, disrupting photosynthesis and causing a collapse of food chains worldwide. The discovery of a worldwide layer of sediment enriched with iridium, an element rare on Earth's surface but common in asteroids, provided crucial evidence supporting this impact hypothesis.","question":"What evidence supports the asteroid impact hypothesis for dinosaur extinction?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Ancient cave paintings depicting the impact event","isCorrect":false},{"id":"b","text":"A worldwide layer of iridium-enriched sediment at the geological boundary","isCorrect":true},{"id":"c","text":"Fossilized asteroid fragments found inside dinosaur bones","isCorrect":false},{"id":"d","text":"Written records from early mammals describing the catastrophe","isCorrect":false}]},{"id":"MCSA-R-047","type":"MCSA-R","skill":"reading","difficulty":4,"timeLimit":120,"instructions":"阅读文章并选择唯一正确答案。","tags":["economics"],"passage":"The concept of universal basic income proposes that all citizens receive regular, unconditional cash payments from the government regardless of their employment status or financial need. Proponents argue it could provide economic security in an era of increasing automation, reduce the bureaucracy associated with means-tested welfare programs, and give individuals greater freedom to pursue education, caregiving, or entrepreneurial ventures. Critics contend that UBI would be prohibitively expensive, could disincentivize work, and might fail to address deeper structural inequalities. Pilot programs in countries including Finland, Kenya, and Canada have produced mixed results, with recipients generally reporting improved well-being and mental health but showing variable effects on employment rates.","question":"What concern about universal basic income do critics raise?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"UBI would be too easy to administer and eliminate all government jobs","isCorrect":false},{"id":"b","text":"UBI could be prohibitively expensive and might disincentivize work","isCorrect":true},{"id":"c","text":"UBI has never been tested in any country anywhere in the world","isCorrect":false},{"id":"d","text":"UBI recipients universally experience declining well-being and mental health","isCorrect":false}]},{"id":"MCSA-R-048","type":"MCSA-R","skill":"reading","difficulty":2,"timeLimit":120,"instructions":"阅读文章并选择唯一正确答案。","tags":["literature"],"passage":"Haiku is a traditional form of Japanese poetry consisting of three lines with a syllable pattern of five, seven, and five. While the form appears deceptively simple, great haiku achieve profound depth through precise observation, juxtaposition of images, and evocation of the natural world's fleeting beauty. The most celebrated haiku poet, Matsuo Basho, elevated the form in the 17th century, infusing it with Zen Buddhist sensibilities of impermanence and mindful attention to the present moment. Haiku has since been adopted and adapted by poets worldwide, becoming one of the most widely practiced poetic forms across cultures and languages.","question":"What makes great haiku profound despite its simple form?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The use of complex rhyming schemes and elaborate metaphors","isCorrect":false},{"id":"b","text":"Precise observation, juxtaposition of images, and evocation of nature's fleeting beauty","isCorrect":true},{"id":"c","text":"Lengthy narrative structures that develop over multiple stanzas","isCorrect":false},{"id":"d","text":"Strict adherence to political themes and social commentary","isCorrect":false}]},{"id":"MCSA-R-049","type":"MCSA-R","skill":"reading","difficulty":3,"timeLimit":120,"instructions":"阅读文章并选择唯一正确答案。","tags":["information technology"],"passage":"Open source software development represents a radical departure from proprietary models of software production. In open source projects, source code is made publicly available, allowing anyone to inspect, modify, and distribute it, typically under licenses that ensure these freedoms are preserved. This collaborative approach has produced some of the world's most widely used software, including the Linux operating system, the Apache web server, and the Python programming language. The success of open source challenges traditional assumptions about the necessity of exclusive property rights for innovation, demonstrating that distributed communities of volunteers can create complex, high-quality technical products without conventional market incentives or hierarchical management structures.","question":"What traditional assumption does open source software challenge?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The assumption that software must be written in programming languages","isCorrect":false},{"id":"b","text":"The necessity of exclusive property rights for innovation, as volunteer communities can create complex, high-quality products","isCorrect":true},{"id":"c","text":"The belief that software can be used on multiple types of computer hardware","isCorrect":false},{"id":"d","text":"The concept that source code must be written in English characters","isCorrect":false}]},{"id":"MCSA-R-050","type":"MCSA-R","skill":"reading","difficulty":4,"timeLimit":120,"instructions":"阅读文章并选择唯一正确答案。","tags":["climatology","history"],"passage":"The Little Ice Age was a period of cooling that occurred between roughly 1300 and 1850 CE, following the Medieval Warm Period. While not a true ice age in the geological sense, this interval brought significantly colder winters to Europe and North America, with rivers such as the Thames in London and the Hudson in New York freezing regularly. The causes remain debated but likely include a combination of reduced solar activity, increased volcanic activity injecting reflective particles into the atmosphere, and changes in ocean circulation patterns. The Little Ice Age had profound historical consequences, contributing to harvest failures, famine, and social unrest, and its end coincided with the onset of the Industrial Revolution and the beginning of anthropogenic climate change.","question":"What factors are cited as likely causes of the Little Ice Age?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Exclusively human industrial activity during the medieval period","isCorrect":false},{"id":"b","text":"A combination of reduced solar activity, increased volcanism, and changes in ocean circulation","isCorrect":true},{"id":"c","text":"A single massive volcanic eruption that darkened the skies for centuries","isCorrect":false},{"id":"d","text":"The complete absence of carbon dioxide in the atmosphere during this period","isCorrect":false}]}]`);export{e as default};